==========================================================================
THE PROBLEM: min-max normalises against whoever happens to be in the set
==========================================================================

  two candidates {A,B}
    B: 0.6250
    A: 0.3750
    ranking: B > A   gap(A,B) = 0.2500

  add irrelevant C  {A,B,C}
    A: 0.8733
    B: 0.7500
    C: 0.0667
    ranking: A > B > C   gap(A,B) = 0.1233

  Two defects visible:
   1. MAGNITUDE IS DESTROYED. slo 0.97 vs 0.99 -- a 2-point difference --
      normalises to 0.000 vs 1.000, identical to what 0.10 vs 0.99 would give.
      The model cannot tell a photo-finish from a landslide.
   2. RANK REVERSAL RISK. Adding a candidate that wins nothing still changes
      every existing score, because it moves the min/max anchors.

==========================================================================
THE FIX: normalise against absolute, spec-derived anchors
==========================================================================

  two candidates {A,B}
    A: 0.7251
    B: 0.7211
    ranking: A > B   gap(A,B) = 0.0040

  add irrelevant C  {A,B,C}
    A: 0.7251
    B: 0.7211
    C: 0.2481
    ranking: A > B > C   gap(A,B) = 0.0040

  A's score with C absent : 0.7251
  A's score with C present: 0.7251   UNCHANGED - immune to rank reversal
  gap(A,B) is now 0.0040, not 0.2500 -- a photo finish
  now LOOKS like a photo finish, which is the point.

  Cost: someone must author the anchors. That is a feature, not a bug --
  the anchors are business facts ('1x headroom is the floor, 5x is plenty')
  and belong in the spec, reviewed, rather than emerging from an accident
  of which candidates were tested.
